<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Babri Masjid</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1528">1528</a>–<a href="/years/1992">1992</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Babri Masjid was a mosque in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayodhya">Ayodhya</a> in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, built in 1528–1529 under the Mughal emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babur">Babur</a>. Many <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindus</a> believed it stood on the birthplace of the god <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rama">Rama</a>, and a long campaign by Hindu nationalist organisations culminated on 6 December 1992 in its demolition by a large crowd, followed by riots in which about 2,000 people died. In 2019 the Supreme Court awarded the site for a Hindu temple.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/babri-masjid.jpg" alt="Babri Masjid" width="320" height="230" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Samuel Bourne[1][2] (1834–1912) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Babri_Masjid_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The name Babri Masjid means Mosque of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babur">Babur</a>. Although many say that Babur ordered its construction, experts agree that his general Mir Baqi did so. Before the 1940s it was called Masjid-i Janmasthan, the mosque of the birthplace, including in official documents. Its architecture followed the school of the Jaunpur Sultanate, and seen from the west it resembled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atala-mosque">Atala Masjid</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jaunpur">Jaunpur</a>.</p><p>The earliest record of a mosque at the site traditionally believed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindus</a> to be the birthplace of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rama">Rama</a> comes from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jai-singh-ii">Jai Singh II</a>, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajputs">Rajput</a> noble at the Mughal court, whose papers include a sketch map of 1717 showing a courtyard labelled janmasthan beside a building with three domes. In 1767 the Jesuit Joseph Tiefenthaler noted that Hindus continued to pray at a mud platform marking the birthplace.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Discovery and study</h2><p>From the mid-19th century the site was disputed between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> and Muslim communities. In 1853 armed ascetics from the Hanuman Garhi temple occupied the mosque, and after clashes in 1855 a boundary wall divided the premises into two courtyards, the Muslims praying in the inner one. On the night of 22 to 23 December 1949 a group of 50 to 60 people placed idols of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rama">Rama</a> inside the mosque, and the government declared it a disputed area and locked its gates. Court cases followed from 1950.</p><p>In 2003, by order of a court, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/archaeological-survey-of-india">Archaeological Survey of India</a> excavated beneath the rubble from 12 March to 7 August and reported what it took to be the remains of a 10th-century shrine under the mosque. Muslim groups and some archaeologists disputed the findings, including the claimed pillar bases.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>In April 1984 the Vishwa Hindu Parishad began a campaign for Hindu access to the site, and in 1986 the locks were removed. On 6 December 1992 leaders and volunteers of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bharatiya-janata-party">Bharatiya Janata Party</a>, the Vishwa Hindu Parishad and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rashtriya-swayamsevak-sangh">Rashtriya Swayamsevak Sangh</a> gathered at the site, and a large crowd demolished the mosque. Riots followed across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-subcontinent">Indian subcontinent</a>, extending to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bangladesh">Bangladesh</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a>.</p><p>The Allahabad High Court ruled in 2010 that the site be divided in three, and on 9 November 2019 a five-judge bench of the Supreme Court ordered the land handed to a trust to build a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> temple and gave an alternative plot at Dhannipur to the Uttar Pradesh Sunni Central Waqf Board for a mosque.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Babri Masjid” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The destruction of the <b>Babri Masjid</b> at Ayodhya in December 1992 and the riots that followed, and the Gujarat violence of 2002, marked the rise of Hindu nationalism as a central force in politics.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-india">Republic of India · Into the Present</a></li></ul></section>
